<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>io.flowcatalyst</groupId>
    <artifactId>flowcatalyst-parent</artifactId>
    <version>0.0.1-SNAPSHOT</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>function-hello</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <name>FlowCatalyst Sample Function — hello</name>
  <description>
    docs/spec/function-developer-surface.md §3 (package E, slice E3): the reference
    function a developer reads before writing their own. One `webhook` endpoint (parses
    an inbound event, reads config + a secret, emits an owned event), one `platform`
    endpoint (reads the caller's principal) and one `none` endpoint (a bare health
    check). Shades in ONE small library (jackson-databind, already in this repo's own
    dependencyManagement) so the build's ProGuard shrink step is exercised for real
    rather than shrinking an empty jar. Only reachable via the `examples` Maven profile
    (root pom.xml) — never part of the default reactor.

    Build shape (docs/function-runner-plan.md §8, "Shrinking"; ORCHESTRATOR RULING
    2026-09-20 — see the module's own git history / final report for the full
    reasoning): this machine's JDK (a mise-managed Temurin 25.0.4, and every other
    Temurin build checked) ships NO `jmods/` directory (JEP 493, "Linking Run-Time
    Images without JMODs"), and ProGuard's own library-jar reading has no fallback
    that works without one — so ProGuard cannot run here, and function authors are
    expected to be on the same class of JDK. The DEFAULT shrinker is therefore
    `maven-shade-plugin`'s own `minimizeJar` (named as the class-granular
    alternative by function-runner-plan.md §8 itself): it needs no JDK class files
    at all — it works from the dependency jars' own bytecode — and runs anywhere.

      compile (release 21, no --enable-preview — same reasoning as function-api/pom.xml:
        a function author's own toolchain compiles against this, not ours)
      -> maven-shade-plugin, minimizeJar=true (shades jackson-databind + its
         transitive deps in, then drops whichever of THEIR classes nothing
         reachable from this module's own compiled classes references;
         `minimizeJar` never touches the project's OWN classes — only a
         dependency's — so HelloFunction/GreetingPayload are always kept whole,
         with none of ProGuard's library-interface-boundary problem. function-api
         stays `provided`, so the shade plugin — which only bundles compile/runtime
         scope — never puts it in the jar; JvmFunctionLoader's BUNDLES_API scan and
         this module's own ShrunkJarTest both prove that) writing directly to
         `${finalName}-shrunk.jar` — no second shrink step in the default build.
      -> the tests run against the SHRUNK jar (E9): a surefire execution bound to the
         `verify` phase (this repo has no maven-failsafe-plugin configured anywhere —
         checked before choosing this shape) loads `target/*-shrunk.jar` through the
         REAL `io.flowcatalyst.fnhost.load.JvmFunctionLoader` and invokes every
         endpoint. The default `test`-phase execution is skipped entirely: the only
         test class needs a jar that `package` has not built yet at that point in the
         lifecycle, so running it there would just fail to find the file.

    ProGuard stays available, OPT-IN, as the `shrink-proguard` Maven profile below —
    unchanged shrink-only config, sharing `proguard.conf` — for a JDK that still
    ships `jmods/` (GraalVM 25, or a Temurin ≤ 23). It is UNVERIFIED BY MAVEN on
    this machine (cannot be: no `jmods/` to read); it was last verified directly via
    the ProGuard CLI on a GraalVM 25 JDK — see the module's final report for that
    run's findings (a clean shrink; the entrypoint needs `-keep class … { *; }`
    because the host calls it through the `Function` interface, a LIBRARY type from
    ProGuard's point of view once `function-api` is `-libraryjars`, and ProGuard's
    reachability analysis cannot see across a library-into-program call it does not
    itself drive).

    Plugin versions, pinned here (not in the parent's pluginManagement — this is the
    only module that uses them) and why:
      - com.github.wvengen:proguard-maven-plugin:2.7.0 — the newest release on Maven
        Central as of this writing (2.7.1 exists only as a SNAPSHOT); the "successor of
        the pyx4me ProGuard Maven Plugin" and the actively maintained one.
      - com.guardsquare:proguard-base:7.10.0 — the newest release on Maven Central as
        of this writing; passed as an explicit plugin dependency element (the wvengen
        plugin does not bundle a ProGuard version itself; see the plugin's own
        proguardVersion mechanism, unused here since we just pin the dependency).
      - maven-shade-plugin: the version already managed by the root pom
        (`pluginManagement`, currently 3.6.2) — reused, not re-pinned, so this module
        never drifts from the version the rest of the repo already trusts.
  </description>

  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.flowcatalyst</groupId>
      <artifactId>flowcatalyst-function-api</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
      <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <!-- Jackson 3 (tools.jackson) — version resolved from the root pom's
           jackson-bom import (dependencyManagement), not repeated here. The ONE
           library this sample shades in, per the design's own instruction: small
           but real, so the ProGuard shrink step in this build actually shrinks
           something. -->
      <groupId>tools.jackson.core</groupId>
      <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Test-only: loads the shrunk jar through the REAL host loader (E9). Pulls
         `flowcatalyst-server` transitively at test scope (Manifest.parseStrict,
         FunctionAddress, Runtime, FunctionLimits, ClientCeilings) — CLAUDE.md build
         hygiene: resolved from the reactor's own target/classes via `-am`, never
         installed. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.flowcatalyst</groupId>
      <artifactId>flowcatalyst-function-host</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
      <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.assertj</groupId>
      <artifactId>assertj-core</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <!-- Overrides the parent's release-25 / enable-preview flags — same reasoning
           and same shape as function-api/pom.xml: this is a function author's own
           build, on their own toolchain, never ours. -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <release>21</release>
          <failOnWarning>${maven.compiler.failOnWarning}</failOnWarning>
          <compilerArgs combine.self="override">
            <arg>-Xlint:all</arg>
            <arg>-parameters</arg>
          </compilerArgs>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>

      <!-- DEFAULT shrinker (ORCHESTRATOR RULING 2026-09-20): shade jackson-databind
           (+ its transitive tools.jackson/jackson-annotations deps) into the main
           jar AND minimize in the same pass — `minimizeJar` removes whichever of
           THOSE dependency classes nothing reachable from this module's own
           compiled classes (and their own transitive reachability inside the
           dependency jars) references; it never touches this module's own classes,
           so HelloFunction/GreetingPayload are always kept whole. function-api is
           `provided` scope, so the shade plugin (compile+runtime scope only) never
           bundles it — ShrunkJarTest proves this against the REAL output, it is not
           just asserted here in a comment. Writes straight to
           `${finalName}-shrunk.jar` (the `outputFile` override below) so the
           default build needs no second shrink step at all — that filename is also
           what `shrink-proguard` produces, so ShrunkJarTest and everything
           downstream is unaware which shrinker built it. -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-shade-plugin</artifactId>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>shade-dependencies</id>
            <phase>package</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>shade</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <createDependencyReducedPom>false</createDependencyReducedPom>
              <minimizeJar>true</minimizeJar>
              <outputFile>${project.build.directory}/${project.build.finalName}-shrunk.jar</outputFile>
              <filters>
                <filter>
                  <artifact>*:*</artifact>
                  <excludes>
                    <exclude>META-INF/*.SF</exclude>
                    <exclude>META-INF/*.DSA</exclude>
                    <exclude>META-INF/*.RSA</exclude>
                    <exclude>META-INF/*.EC</exclude>
                    <exclude>module-info.class</exclude>
                    <exclude>META-INF/versions/*/module-info.class</exclude>
                  </excludes>
                </filter>
              </filters>
              <transformers>
                <transformer implementation="org.apache.maven.plugins.shade.resource.ServicesResourceTransformer"/>
              </transformers>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>

      <!-- E9: the tests run against the SHRUNK jar. This repo has no
           maven-failsafe-plugin anywhere (checked: `grep -rl maven-failsafe-plugin`
           over every pom.xml finds nothing) — introducing it for one module is a
           bigger footprint than reusing surefire with a second, later-bound
           execution, so: the default `test`-phase run is skipped outright (the one
           test class this module has needs a jar that `package` has not built yet
           at that point — the shrunk jar, whichever shrinker built it, does not
           exist before `package` completes), and a second execution, bound to
           `verify` (strictly after `package`, so after the shade+minimizeJar
           execution above — or, under `shrink-proguard`, after shade AND ProGuard
           — have both run), runs it for real. `mvn verify` is therefore the command
           that proves anything; `mvn test` alone proves nothing in this module and
           is expected to report zero tests run. -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <skipTests>true</skipTests>
        </configuration>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>shrunk-jar-test</id>
            <phase>verify</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>test</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <skipTests>false</skipTests>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>

  <!-- OPT-IN alternative shrinker (ORCHESTRATOR RULING 2026-09-20, see this module's
       description block above): ProGuard, unchanged from before this ruling, for a
       JDK that still ships `jmods/` (GraalVM 25, or a Temurin ≤ 23) — this machine's
       JDK does not, so `-Pshrink-proguard` cannot be verified by Maven here; it was
       last verified directly via the ProGuard CLI on GraalVM 25 (see the final
       report). Activate with `-Pexamples,shrink-proguard`. -->
  <profiles>
    <profile>
      <id>shrink-proguard</id>
      <build>
        <plugins>
          <!-- Overrides the `shade-dependencies` execution above: same filters and
               ServicesResourceTransformer (inherited unchanged — a profile's
               <configuration> merges onto the base plugin's per matching execution
               id, dominant on elements it redeclares, so only `minimizeJar` and
               `outputFile` need repeating here), but shades WITHOUT minimizing, and
               writes to the plain `${finalName}.jar` — ProGuard's own `<injar>`
               below — instead of straight to `-shrunk.jar`, so ProGuard (not
               minimizeJar) is what actually shrinks the artifact under this
               profile. -->
          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
            <artifactId>maven-shade-plugin</artifactId>
            <executions>
              <execution>
                <id>shade-dependencies</id>
                <configuration>
                  <minimizeJar>false</minimizeJar>
                  <outputFile>${project.build.directory}/${project.build.finalName}.jar</outputFile>
                </configuration>
              </execution>
            </executions>
          </plugin>

          <!-- ProGuard, shrink-only (docs/function-runner-plan.md §8): reads
               proguard.conf (`-dontobfuscate -dontoptimize` + the keep rules) beside
               manifest.json. `includeDependency` (plugin default: true) adds this
               module's own compile-scope dependencies — including `function-api`,
               `provided` is on the Maven "compile" resolution scope the plugin resolves
               against — as `-libraryjars` automatically. The JDK's own classes are NOT
               configured here as a `<libs>` entry: ProGuard's own `<java.home>` macro
               (documented in its manual as text substituted into a *path you still write
               the rest of*, e.g. `<java.home>/jmods/java.base.jmod` — NOT a self-contained
               "read the whole run-time image" directive; there is no such directive in
               proguard-core 9.4.0/9.1.10, confirmed by inspecting both jars directly: zero
               classes anywhere under `proguard.io` reference `jrt:` or a JEP 220 run-time
               image) is a MAVEN property collision waiting to happen if written inside
               pom.xml (`${java.home}` would be resolved by Maven itself first), so this
               whole directive lives in `proguard.conf` instead, verbatim, where ProGuard
               reads it as raw, un-interpolated text.

               KNOWN ENVIRONMENT BLOCKER, reported rather than worked around (see the
               final report; this repeats CLAUDE.md/the brief's own instruction for exactly
               this situation): `<java.home>/jmods/*.jmod` is the ONLY input format
               proguard-core actually supports for a modular JDK's own classes, and this
               build's own JDK — a mise-managed Temurin 25.0.4.1, but ALSO the raw,
               official upstream Adoptium tarball for both macOS/aarch64 and linux/x64,
               downloaded and inspected directly — ships NO `jmods/` directory at all
               (plausibly JEP 493, "Linking Run-Time Images without JMODs": jlink no
               longer needs jmods to build a run-time image, so recent Temurin builds stop
               shipping them — proguard-core's own library-jar reading was never updated
               for that). Fixing this needs either a third build plugin (`exec-maven-
               plugin`, to run `jimage extract` first) or a different JDK toolchain for
               this one step (e.g. this repo's own GraalVM install, which does ship
               jmods) — both outside what this profile is authorised to add/require on
               its own, which is exactly why it is opt-in rather than the default. -->
          <plugin>
            <groupId>com.github.wvengen</groupId>
            <artifactId>proguard-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            <version>2.7.0</version>
            <executions>
              <execution>
                <phase>package</phase>
                <goals>
                  <goal>proguard</goal>
                </goals>
              </execution>
            </executions>
            <configuration>
              <injar>${project.build.finalName}.jar</injar>
              <outjar>${project.build.finalName}-shrunk.jar</outjar>
              <obfuscate>false</obfuscate>
              <proguardInclude>${project.basedir}/proguard.conf</proguardInclude>
            </configuration>
            <dependencies>
              <!-- proguard-maven-plugin 2.7.0's OWN pom declares proguard-core:9.1.10
                   directly (optional, runtime) — NEARER in Maven's dependency graph than
                   proguard-base 7.10.0's own transitive proguard-core:9.4.0, so mediation
                   picks the OLDER, plugin-declared 9.1.10 unless overridden explicitly
                   here too (confirmed directly: without this, ~/.m2 held both jars and
                   9.1.10 silently won). Pinned to the SAME 9.4.0 proguard-base 7.10.0
                   was built and tested against. -->
              <dependency>
                <groupId>com.guardsquare</groupId>
                <artifactId>proguard-core</artifactId>
                <version>9.4.0</version>
                <scope>runtime</scope>
              </dependency>
              <dependency>
                <groupId>com.guardsquare</groupId>
                <artifactId>proguard-base</artifactId>
                <version>7.10.0</version>
                <scope>runtime</scope>
              </dependency>
            </dependencies>
          </plugin>
        </plugins>
      </build>
    </profile>
  </profiles>
</project>
